   [{:id "755100a6-034b-4de4-9711-23d981cf2ff4"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-assumption"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Assumption"
     :text
     "a foundation declared provisionally: a supposition granted for the sake of the reasoning, without claiming it is true.\n\nIt is not refuted directly: you relax it or swap in a different supposition, then watch how the conclusions that depend on it are affected."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-08-15T12:14:21Z"}
    {:id "ac1c3dbf-3327-41e8-b5b7-6695b601e0f7"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-belief"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Belief"
     :text
     "a foundation you hold as given and stand by, whatever the register — personal, philosophical, civic or formal.\n\nIt is not refuted by counterexample: a belief is contested only by an incompatible belief, and the platform keeps the rival variants with their objection histories rather than forcing a resolution that does not exist."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-08-15T12:14:21Z"}
    {:id "93190138-f346-4faa-b2f4-e64b2aaecb14"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-counter-example"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Counter-example"
     :text
     "a single instance that refutes a general claim by contradicting it: if a claim asserts 'all X are Y', one X that is not Y shows it false — or at least not precise enough. It targets another claim and withdraws its generality.\n\nIt is itself challenged on two grounds: whether the instance is real (is Einstein actually smart?) and whether it truly falls under the claim (is Einstein one of 'all people'?). A sound counter-example forces the target to narrow, qualify, or be withdrawn."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-08-15T12:14:21Z"}
    {:id "3a0f4c86-7091-4be3-90c4-6504493d5451"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-deduction"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Deduction"
     :text
     "a deductive step: from inputs held to be true, the conclusion follows *necessarily*.\n\nThe only admissible challenges are that the form is invalid, or that a term is used equivocally — you cannot accept the premises and the form and yet refuse the conclusion."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-08-17T13:21:44Z"}
    {:id "291a803c-e180-439d-bfd6-1818cb06da82"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-definition"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Definition"
     :text
     "a semantic contract that fixes the meaning of a term in the graph; the key terms of other statements link to it.\n\nA definition is not contested in itself: you set a different definition of the same word against it. The graph then surfaces the ambiguity — several competing definitions for one term — instead of deciding."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-08-15T12:14:21Z"}
    {:id "cd3a8dcd-e56c-4202-951d-dfcddbae6de2"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-article"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Article"
     :text
     "an article that lays out and links the graph's claims into readable prose — an entry point that makes the reasoning legible for a newcomer, informing and explaining rather than advocating or calling to act.\n\nIt is not challenged like a single reasoning step: an explainer rests on the claims it cites, so a disagreement is taken up with those; the article itself can still be discussed for its clarity, balance or what it leaves out."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-08-15T12:14:21Z"}
    {:id "fb385b44-99a3-4314-b9e0-ab0316ee2e25"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-extract"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Extract"
     :text
     "a passage drawn verbatim from a work at a precise location (page, chapter, verse): it cites the one work it comes from and pins the locator, so the appropriations built on it can point back to exactly what was said.\n\nIt is contested on fidelity: whether the passage is transcribed accurately, at the stated location, in the edition cited."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-08-15T12:14:21Z"}
    {:id "86cb547d-b995-4be6-98bc-2d7ac1f259e2"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-illustration"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Illustration"
     :text
     "a concrete example that makes another claim vivid — it shows an instance, it does not derive anything: no reasoning depends on it and nothing follows from it.\n\nIt is not challenged as reasoning: an illustration is apt or inapt, representative or misleading, but it proves nothing on its own — one disputes whether it truly exemplifies the claim, not whether it is 'valid'."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-08-15T12:14:21Z"}
    {:id "bd3ee4d9-9f71-4ace-82ca-e5402f47e952"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-induction"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Induction"
     :text
     "an ampliative step: the conclusion goes *beyond* what the inputs strictly guarantee.\n\nYou may accept the premises, accept the form, and still find the leap too wide — here the challenge is a judgment on the leap itself, not a matter of formal validity."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-08-17T13:21:44Z"}
    {:id "3730889c-868c-4311-9f45-6c0db053742e"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-measurable-fact"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Measurable fact"
     :text
     "an empirical foundation: a statement settled by a reproducible measurement or observation, holding independently of who asserts it.\n\nIt is not contested by reasoning but on the measurement itself — the protocol, the instrument, its reproducibility — or by producing a measurement that contradicts it."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-08-15T15:55:44Z"}
    {:id "004756b2-9f88-4b54-ba9a-00af660a5abb"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-prediction"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Prediction"
     :text
     "verifiable knowledge whose truth will be settled by an observable event, possibly in the future.\n\nBefore resolution, objections bear on the soundness of the reasoning; at resolution, reality decides and the debate closes — confirmed or refuted, whatever the community's confidence."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-08-15T12:14:21Z"}
    {:id "391106f6-41a5-489a-a11d-6e0f3a87c34c"
     :type :definition
     :name "type-work"
     :lang :en
     :major 1
     :minor 0
     :title "Work"
     :text
     "an external document cited as bibliographic provenance — a book, article, dictionary, page… — shared (title, author, year, editor, URL) and reused by every passage drawn from it.\n\nIt is not contested like a piece of reasoning: you can discuss its accuracy, the edition chosen, or the attribution, but the work itself remains a shared provenance."
     :inputs []
     :published-at "2026-10-18T09:16:38Z"}]}
